## 1. Files

A program file ends in `.oo`.
A process doc ends in `.oot`.
This wiki is `.oot`. The code you compile is `.oo`.

```
hello.oo                 program
guide/Language.oot       this page
```

## 2. A small program

Save this as `hello.oo`:

```
pub fn greet(name: String) -> String {
    return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

verify greet {
    assert_eq!(greet("World"), "Hello, World!");
}

pub fn main() {
    println(greet("openOODA"));
}
```

`pub fn` is a public function.
`verify` is a test block next to the function it names.
`assert_eq!` and `assert!` live in `verify`.

```
ooda check hello.oo
ooda run hello.oo
ooda test hello.oo
```

`ooda check` typechecks. It does not run.
`ooda run` prints the greeting and skips `verify`.
`ooda test` runs `verify` / `assert_eq!` / `assert!`.

First run: [Start](Use.oot).
The product path: [How it works](Limits.oot).

## 3. Option, Result, and nested Result

`Int`, `String`, `Bool`, `Float`, and `Void` are the primitives.
`Option[Int]` and `Result[Int]` are sum types check tracks.
`type Name = struct { ... }` names a record.
`List[Name]` and `List[Float]` emit. One-level `List[List[Int]]` emits. Deeper nests stay residual.

`Result[List[Name], Error]` is a Result whose Ok payload is a list of records.
Handle it with `match` or `match let`. Do not discard it.

Unused `Ok`, `Err`, or `Some` fail `ooda check`.
A discarded `Result` fails `ooda check`.

```
pub fn boom() -> Result[String, String] {
    return Ok("yes");
}

pub fn main() {
    boom();
}
```

That `boom();` fails check. Bind the value, `match` it, or use `?`.

`ooda fix` can wrap a discarded `Result`. See [Commands](Options.oot).

## 4. match, match let, and ?

`match` must be exhaustive.
Every constructor the type names must appear.
An annotated `Option` or `Result` match whose only arm is `_` is refused.

```
type Name = struct { s: String };
type Error = struct { msg: String };

pub fn label(r: Result[List[Name], Error]) -> String {
    return match r {
        Ok(names) => "ok",
        Err(err) => err.msg,
    };
}

pub fn main() {
    match let x = load_names() {
        Ok(names) => {
            println("ok");
        },
        Err(e) => {
            println(e.msg);
        }
    }
}
```

`?` is the try postfix. It sits after a call.

```
pub fn main() {
    let y = boom()?;
    println(y);
}
```

## 5. Struct methods

A method takes a receiver. Call it on a value or on a borrow.

```
type Point = struct { x: Int, y: Int };

pub fn scale(self: Point) -> Point {
    return Point { x: self.x * 10, y: self.y * 10 };
}

pub fn shift_x(pt: &Point, dx: Int) -> Int {
    return pt.x + dx;
}

pub fn main() {
    let p = Point { x: 2, y: 3 };
    let q = p.scale();
    let n = (&p).shift_x(1);
    println(n);
}
```

Call `p.scale()` on the value. Call `(&p).shift_x()` on a borrow.
The borrowed receiver is `pt: &Point`.

## 6. Inline if and for ranges

`if` is an expression. You may add its value.

```
pub fn pick(val: Int, cond: Bool, a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
    let y = val + (if cond { a } else { b });
    return y;
}
```

`for x in xs` walks a list.
`for i in 0..n` walks a range.
The high bound may be an expression:

```
pub fn sum_prefix(n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut t = 0;
    for i in 0..(n - 1) {
        t = t + i;
    }
    return t;
}
```

## 7. Contracts

A function header may carry `requires`, `ensures`, and `spec`.
Those clauses can fold at check time.

```
pub fn held(x: Int) -> Int
    requires 1 == 1
    ensures 2 == 2
    spec 1 != 0
{
    return x;
}
```

A false const clause fails the check. You do not wait for run.
Full SMT stays residual.

## 8. Capability tokens

An effect takes a token argument, for example `&FsReadCap`.
No token, no effect.

```
pub fn exists(fs: &FsReadCap, path: String) -> Bool {
    return path_exists(fs, path);
}
```

Prefer the small token.
`&FsReadCap` covers `read_file`, `path_exists`, and `file_size`.

The token table: [Commands](Options.oot).
How tokens work: [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

## 9. SECRET taint

Mark a name secret. Printing a secret field is refused.
The sibling field that is not secret is allowed.

```
type Creds = struct { token: String, name: String };
// SECRET: token

pub fn main() {
    let c = Creds { token: "s3cret", name: "alice" };
    println(c.name);
}
```

`println(c.token)` fails check. `process_exec` is a secret sink.
Landlock, HMAC attenuation, and `SECRET` fields: [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

## 10. Sums and product std

`type Flag = A(Int) | B(String) | Empty` is a tagged sum.
`&mut Name` writes through. `(*p).f = rhs` writes the field.

Product std is `ooda/std`. Import from that tree.
How you add more code: [Packages](Packages.oot).

```
import "std/fs/path.oo";

pub fn main() {
    println(path_join("ooda", "std"));
}
```

A std function that does an effect still takes a token.
Std does not grant ambient authority.

